# Waters Connect Cloud multi-repo workspace

This workspace root is **not** a git repository and has **no** `.beads` database, but the
`wcc-ai-guidance` skills (`plan-issue`, `implement-issue`, `review-issues`) each assume a
single repo root. The overrides below make them work across repos; they win wherever they
conflict with the skill text, and everything else in those skills applies unchanged.

When this rule is triggered via an agent, inform the user that it has been triggered and prompt them to confirm whether it should have been.
- If the user confirms, proceed with the multi-repo workflow.
- Otherwise, unload the rule and carry on as if it had not been triggered.

## Scope

Only repositories under `wcc/` participate. Ignore `obsidian-notes/`, `windows-config/`,
`buku-bookmarks/`, and `adam-nx-poc-v3/` unless explicitly asked. Each repo carries its own
`graphify-out/` — read the one inside the target repo; there is no workspace-wide graph.

## Ticket topology

A cross-repo ticket has exactly one **lead repo** and a list of **participating repos**:

- `$LEAD_REPO` — the repo owning the ticket's primary change. It hosts all beads for the ticket.
- `$REPOS` — every repo the ticket touches, lead included.

Decide both during `plan-issue` Step 3 and confirm them with the user before creating anything.

## Bead coordination contract

All beads for the ticket live in **one** database — the lead repo's. Reach it with an
absolute `-C` path, so the same command works from the root and from inside any subagent's
worktree:

```bash
LEAD_ROOT="/home/ukafig/repos/wcc/$LEAD_REPO"
bd -C "$LEAD_ROOT" <command>
```

Prefix **every** `bd` call in every skill this way. Then:

- Parent bead carries `--external-ref jira-<KEY>` (unchanged) plus
  `--set-metadata lead=<lead-repo>` and `--set-metadata repos="<comma-separated list>"`.
- Every child bead names exactly one repo at creation: `--metadata '{"repo":"<repo>"}'`.
  Split work spanning two repos into two beads.
- A child's ID comes from the lead's database, so its `repo` key is the only reliable
  signal of where its code lives. Read it before touching any file.
- Never run `bd init` at the workspace root — there must be no `/home/ukafig/repos/.beads`.

`bd context` resolves the git root from the original working directory and ignores `-C`, so
it fails here. `cd` into the repo for that one command.

## plan-issue overrides

- **Branch name (Steps 4-5)** — derive **one** name and reuse it verbatim in every
  participating repo. Record it once with `--set-metadata branch=<name>`, so
  `implement-issue`'s single `.metadata.branch` read resolves correctly in all of them.
- **Design (Step 8)** — state which repo owns which part of the change, so each child bead
  can name a single repo.
- **Test plan (Step 10a)** — no repo under `wcc/` has `.agents/manifest.yaml`, so do **not**
  read `component_tier`: it resolves to the global `~/.agents/manifest.yaml`
  (`domain_service_tier2`) and is wrong for SPA and library repos. Detect per repo by shape:

  | Signal in `wcc/<repo>` | Tier |
  |---|---|
  | `**/ClientApp/angular.json` | `spa_client`, plus `presentation_api_tier1` for its .NET host |
  | `*.Domain` and `*.Server` projects | `domain_service_tier2` |
  | NuGet library (`wcc-lib-*`, no service host) | Library — Unit and Backward Compatibility only; Component, System Integration and UI layers are N/A |

  Run Steps 10a-10d **once per participating repo** and emit the markers per repo:

  ```
  [TESTING] testing-skills-brain PLAN mode — repo=<repo> <NEW|EXISTING>, tier=<tier> (evidence=suite detection)
  ```

- **Decomposition (Step 11)** — wire cross-repo ordering with `bd dep add` as normal; it
  works because every bead is in the same database.

## implement-issue overrides

- **Step 1** — create one worktree **per participating repo**, each at
  `wcc/<repo>/.worktrees/$BRANCH_NAME`, running the skill's existing fetch and branch logic
  inside that repo. There is no single parent worktree.
- **Step 2a** — capture one `TEST-BASELINE` note per repo on the parent, each labelled with
  its repo name.
- **Step 3a** — read each bead's `repo` metadata and hand the engineer that repo's root plus
  the shared `$BRANCH_NAME`. The engineer's own `REPO_ROOT` resolution and
  `ai/$BRANCH_NAME/<bead-id>-<slug>` convention then work unchanged, because the parent
  branch exists in every repo.
- **Step 3c** — rebase each bead's branch inside **its own** repo's worktree. Never rebase
  across repos.
- **Step 5** — cut one PR per repo that has commits. Record the lead's URL as `pr` so
  `review-issues` keeps working unchanged, and **every** PR URL as a space-separated `prs`
  list. Each URL already contains its repo name, so no per-repo key is needed — metadata
  keys must match `[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_.]*`, which rules out hyphenated repo names:

  ```bash
  bd -C "$LEAD_ROOT" update $PARENT_ID --add-label awaiting-merge \
    --set-metadata pr="$LEAD_PR_URL" \
    --set-metadata prs="$LEAD_PR_URL $OTHER_PR_URL"
  ```

  Every PR body carries the Jira link and the URLs of its sibling PRs.
- **Step 5 cleanup** — sweep worktrees and `ai/*` branches in each repo separately.

## review-issues overrides

Sweep with `bd -C "$LEAD_ROOT"`. Resolve the PR list from `.metadata.prs` (falling back to
`.metadata.pr` for single-repo tickets) and process each URL — `gh pr view <url>` resolves
owner and repo from a full URL, so no `-R` is needed. Close the parent only when **all** PRs
have merged; when some have merged and others are still open, leave it `awaiting-merge` and
report the split.

## Library and consumer ordering

When a ticket spans a `wcc-lib-*` package and a repo consuming it, make the consumer's beads
depend on the library's (`bd dep add <consumer-bead> <lib-bead>`). Do **not** bump the
consumer's `PackageReference` or sequence the merges yourself: open the library PR, then stop
and hand off to a human with the version the consumer will need. Package publication and
merge order are human decisions.

## Guardrails

- Never run `git init`, `bd init`, `git commit`, or create worktrees at the workspace root.
- Every file edit happens inside a worktree under `wcc/<repo>/.worktrees/$BRANCH_NAME`.
- `wcc-authorizationas` has both `current/` and `next/` trees — confirm which one a bead
  targets before editing.
- Never push `ai/*` branches, in any repo.

## Reporting

One Jira comment and one final summary for the whole ticket, grouped by repo:

```
<KEY> — <title>
Lead: <lead-repo>   Branch: <branch-name> (same name in every repo)

<repo>: <bead ids> → <PR url | not started>
<repo>: <bead ids> → <PR url | not started>
```
